---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
	page="frozen"
	title="Frozen paths"
	lede="For files that can't be rewritten after they ship — formatted while new, never touched again."
>
	<section>
		<h2>The problem</h2>
		<p>
			sqlx records a checksum of every migration it has run and refuses to
			start when one of them changes. The checksum is over the bytes, so a
			reformat that means exactly the same thing still breaks it. The same
			shape shows up anywhere a file is hashed after the fact: applied
			migrations, golden files, anything signed.
		</p>
		<p>
			Ignoring the directory is the obvious move, and it is not good enough:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				New migrations should still be formatted. Ignoring{" "}
				<code>migrations/</code> gives that up for the one case where it is
				free.
			</li>
			<li>
				Adopting squill on an existing codebase cannot start with rewriting
				every migration that has already run.
			</li>
			<li>
				Even if the whole directory were in squill&rsquo;s style today,
				squill&rsquo;s style will change, and the next release would rewrite
				files that shipped years ago.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>What frozen does</h2>
		<p>
			A <code>frozen</code> glob marks paths squill formats only while they are
			new. A matching file is skipped once it exists in the baseline ref&rsquo;s
			tree; files that don&rsquo;t exist there yet format normally.
		</p>
		<pre><code>frozen = ["migrations/**"]</code></pre>
		<p>
			So a migration is formatted on the branch that introduces it, and from
			the moment it lands on the baseline it is never rewritten again &mdash;
			not by a later run, and not when squill&rsquo;s own formatting changes
			underneath it. Adopting squill on an old repository freezes every
			already-shipped migration on day one, with no bulk rewrite.
		</p>
		<div class="note">
			<p>
				This is not <code>ignore</code>. A frozen file is skipped even when
				you name it explicitly on the command line, because the point is that
				it is never rewritten &mdash; and a pre-commit hook passes explicit
				paths.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Finding the baseline</h2>
		<p>
			squill asks git what the baseline branch is rather than guessing from a
			list of names, so it works whatever yours is called. In order:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><code>frozen-ref</code>, if you set one.</li>
			<li>
				The remote&rsquo;s recorded HEAD
				(<code>refs/remotes/&lt;remote&gt;/HEAD</code>), which{" "}
				<code>git clone</code> sets and{" "}
				<code>git remote set-head</code> refreshes.
			</li>
			<li>
				The sole remote-tracking branch, when there is exactly one &mdash; the
				shape a CI checkout that fetched one branch leaves behind.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The remote is the only convention left, and only when there is more than
			one: with a single remote squill uses it whatever it is named, falling
			back to <code>origin</code> when several exist.
		</p>
		<p>
			Reading the baseline is one <code>git ls-tree</code> per repository, and
			only the ref&rsquo;s tip tree is needed, never its history. A CI checkout
			at <code>fetch-depth: 1</code> is enough, as long as the base branch
			itself was fetched.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>When there is no baseline</h2>
		<p>
			Some workflows check out a pull request without fetching the base branch
			at all. There squill stops with an error rather than guess, because a
			wrong guess means quietly rewriting a shipped migration &mdash; the exact
			failure the setting exists to prevent. It names three ways out:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><code>git remote set-head &lt;remote&gt; --auto</code> to record it.</li>
			<li><code>frozen-ref = "origin/your-branch"</code> to name it.</li>
			<li>
				<code>--frozen-fetch</code> (or <code>frozen-fetch = true</code>) to
				let squill run a <code>--depth=1</code> fetch of the remote&rsquo;s
				HEAD itself.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Fetching is off by default. It is the only thing squill does that touches
			the network, and a formatter that reaches out implicitly is one that
			hangs on a slow link, fails in an offline build, and needs credentials it
			may not have.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Reading the output</h2>
		<p>
			Skipped files are counted in the summary, so a frozen file is never a
			silent one:
		</p>
		<pre
			class="dark"><code>12 file(s) checked, 1 reformatted, 0 diagnostic(s), 34 frozen</code></pre>
		<p>
			<code>--check</code> ignores them too, so CI does not fail over a
			migration that shipped before squill arrived.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
